<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chand dynasty</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chand dynasty ruled the hill kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, until the Gorkha conquest of 1790. Tradition traces it to Som Chand, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> prince from the plains who settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champawat">Champawat</a>, though its early chronology is legendary. By the 15th and 16th centuries the Chands had subdued rival hill chiefs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-chand">Kalyan Chand</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> around 1563.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chand-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chand dynasty" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyder Young Hearsey (1782-1840) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_and_the_capital_city_of_Kali_Kumaon,_Champawat,_1815.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
